  "Question": "One root of the equation in \\(x\\), \\(x²+kx+6=0\\), is \\(2\\). Find the value of \\(k\\) and the other root. (Answer in order, separated by a comma.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "-5，3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Since the product of the two roots is \\(6\\), the other root is \\(6\\div2=3\\). Their sum is \\(2+3=5=-k\\), so \\(k=-5\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Substitute the known root, then factor\n\nA given root makes the equation zero, so substituting \\(x=2\\) determines \\(k\\):\n\\[\n4+2k+6=0,\\qquad 2k=-10,\\qquad k=-5.\n\\]\nWith this value, the equation becomes\n\\[\nx^2-5x+6=(x-2)(x-3)=0.\n\\]\nIts roots are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), so the other root is \\(3\\). The requested pair is \\(-5,\\,3\\).",
